  {:id :I1 :class :I :status :open :owner :claude-20 :assigned "2026-09-01 by Joe: claude-20 orchestrates under the coding-handoff protocol (Codex implements in small handoffs; claude-20 reviews); registry/ledger rows it produces are read by claude-1"
   :slice-b1 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 a75f763 (codex-21) + 2598fbe (owner review fixes). src/futon2/aif/policy_precision.clj iterates Appendix B discrete relaxation beta_next = beta + step.gamma^2.[(beta_prior - beta) + (pi - pi_0).G] to eq. 2.7 fixed point; pure, wired into nothing. 8 tests / 26 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. F_pi enters UNSCALED, gamma multiplying G alone, per eq. 2.7. beta_prior required, no default. converged?/hit-bound?/iterations returned as data. THE HEADLINE IS NOT WHAT WAS REPORTED. codex-21 table said beta_0 = 2.0 and 5.0 DID NOT CONVERGE at the 256-iteration bound. Recomputed on the same field: ALL FOUR CONVERGE. beta_0 = 2.0 converges at 258 -- a bound two iterations short -- with gamma identical to eight decimals (delta 7.8e-11); beta_0 = 5.0 converges at 1726. Reporting a bound artifact as a property of the problem is the same class as the tau = 1 comparison earlier today: a measurement made where it could not have given the other answer. WHAT THE FIELD ACTUALLY SHOWS, and it is an operational consequence of Joe carried ruling: ITERATIONS GROW STEEPLY WITH BETA -- 5, 24, 258, 1726 for beta_0 = 0.5, 1, 2, 5 -- because the effective step is step.gamma^2 = step/beta^2, so the rate per iteration falls off like 1/beta^2. Under J4 beta IS the previous tick posterior, so a beta that drifts upward makes each tick quadratically slower to solve. Default :max-iterations raised 256 -> 4096, covering the measured 1726 with headroom, with the measurement in the docstring so a caller sets the bound knowingly. SECOND FINDING: :step-size 0.25 is not merely the paper simulation value, it is inside a STABILITY BOUNDARY THAT MOVES WITH BETA. At step 1.0, beta_0 = 0.5 and 1.0 fail to converge in 200,000 iterations and settle on the WRONG gamma (0.319 against the true 2.009); at step 4.0 they diverge. Meanwhile beta_0 = 2 and 5 converge FASTER at the larger steps (57 and 425 at step 1.0). So the usable step depends on the carried beta. Recorded in the docstring with the numbers. THIRD FINDING, fixed: THE GUARD WAS ONE-SIDED. beta -> 0 was floored, because gamma = 1/beta goes infinite. beta -> infinity was unguarded, and gives gamma -> 0 with a near-uniform pi -- a degenerate answer that LOOKS valid. Observed at step 4.0: beta reached 2e12 and gamma 5e-13 with no exception, since those values are finite. :converged? was false so it was not silent, but a caller ignoring the flag gets a plausible number. Added :beta-ceiling (default 1e6) with :beta-ceiling-hit? and a count, symmetric with the floor, and a test that DRIVES the divergence rather than asserting the guard exists. SOLVER REPLACED AFTER claude-1 READ THE ABOVE, and it dissolves the first two findings rather than managing them (futon2 0fb8b27). Their point: eq. 2.7 defines a SCALAR FIXED POINT IN ONE UNKNOWN, and iterating to it is the paper scheme, not a property the object has. Both the 1/beta^2 slowdown and the moving stability boundary follow from stepping in beta with a fixed step, since the effective step is step/beta^2. Checked on the real field before changing anything: the residual beta_prior + (pi(beta) - pi_0(beta)).G - beta is STRICTLY DECREASING with EXACTLY ONE SIGN CHANGE over exp(-6)..exp(6), for every beta_prior in {0.5, 1, 2, 5}. So the root is unique there and bisection reaches it. Measured evaluations, bisect against gradient: 70/5, 70/24, 70/258, 68/1726, 65/171018 at beta_prior 50, and 61 against >200000 at beta_prior 500 where the gradient solver never converges and is wrong in the eighth decimal. The two agree to 1e-9 or better wherever the gradient one converges. BISECTION IS NOW THE DEFAULT; :solver :gradient is retained as the paper stated scheme and as a cross-check. Cost is FLAT IN BETA and slightly cheaper for large beta, so a drifting carried beta does not make later ticks more expensive -- the cost I recorded above as a consequence of J4 was a consequence of one solver. There is no step size, so no stability boundary; the :max-iterations 4096 default I raised is no longer load-bearing for the default path. :beta-floor and :beta-ceiling become the BRACKET rather than clamps, so the guard work is not wasted, and the bracket ends are checked for opposite signs -- :bracketed? false with both end residuals is returned rather than halving to a meaningless midpoint. Monotonicity is a numerical observation on one field, NOT a proof, and the docstring and the bracket check both say so. FOURTH, minor and correct as delivered: the 07-04 record carries :G-total but no persisted prediction fields, so codex-21 derived predictions from the record actions and first-record state with the pure forward model and scored the second observation with {:absent-variance :floor}. Same route the I2(b1) review used, and the right one for a pre-I3 record; noted so nobody later reads these numbers as having come from persisted predictions."
   :statement "Implement policy precision as gamma = 1/beta with the beta update of friston2017 eq. 2.7 (beta <- beta + (pi - pi0) . G), replacing effective-temperature's selection-gain proxy on the live path, behind a flag until compared."
   :acceptance "policy.clj computes tau = 1/gamma from the beta update; a run record shows gamma changing tick to tick from G and pi; the selection-gain path remains available under FUTON_WM_TAU_MODE for comparison; tests; clj-kondo clean; registry :temperature-update :observed updated by a reviewed C row; sha here."}
  {:id :I2 :class :I :status :open :owner :claude-20 :assigned "2026-09-01 by Joe: claude-20 orchestrates under the coding-handoff protocol (Codex implements in small handoffs; claude-20 reviews); registry/ledger rows it produces are read by claude-1"
   :slice-b1 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 764db75 (codex-21) + cb9a7f4 (owner review fix). src/futon2/aif/policy_free_energy.clj computes the horizon-one Gaussian F_pi per candidate; nothing consumes it yet. 8 tests / 19 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. REVIEW FINDING, fixed not re-belled: the delivered function could not be called on WM data at all. It treated every zero variance as a deterministic claim and rejected any mismatch -- but forward-model/predict gives a variance of 0.0 to every channel the action model does not touch, marked :variance-status {:status :absent :reason :deterministic-by-action-model}, which means NO PREDICTION, not a claim to predict exactly. Measured on wm-trace-2026-07-04: 14 channels, 12 absent per candidate, 7 moving between consecutive ticks, so scoring the tick-t prediction against the tick-(t+1) observation threw on :mathematics-pct at residual 0.001. The delivered tests all passed because every fixture was 1-2 synthetic channels; a WM-shaped fixture (2 predicted channels, 3 declared absent) reproduces the rejection, and is now committed. Fix: :absent-variance option, :reject still the default, :floor uses :variance-floor 0.01 -- the same floor futon2.aif.precision applies to its own variance -- for absent channels ONLY; a zero with no :absent status still rejects. No silent default turns a rejection into a number. MEASURED FOR SLICE (b2), so the wiring decision is not taken blind: over the real 110-candidate field of 07-04 scored against the next tick, F_pi takes 19 distinct values with a spread of 0.82 and a largest bucket of 35 candidates sharing one value; G over the same field takes 30 distinct values with a spread of 9.62. B.9 adds ln E - F_pi - G unscaled, so F_pi would move the posterior by roughly a twelfth of what G does. It is a real, discriminating, SMALL correction -- (b2) should report the rank changes it actually causes rather than claiming the term is now present."
   :slice-b2-followup "Three corrections to the b2 review, 2026-09-01, two of them claude-1 findings and one mine. (1) THE SCALING IS RESOLVED BY SOURCE, not by the invariance argument. friston2017 eq. 2.7 is pi = sigma(-F - gamma.G): gamma multiplies G ALONE and F enters unscaled (friston2017.txt:660-666). B.9 has no temperature at all, so eq. 2.7 is the one source that has one, and it puts tau nowhere near F. :unscaled is therefore settled and the :by-tau arm is CLOSED, not open. The tau-invariance property stands as the reason the formalism form is the sensible one, not as the reason for choosing it. (2) THE ARGMAX TEST DOES NOT COVER ENACTMENT, and my summary overclaimed by saying F_pi changes nothing the machine does. On the close-loop! path the enacted action is the first ranked candidate that PASSES ITS ACT GATE (V1/C460: in 50 of 50 comparable records, not the rank-1 selection), so a reshuffle of ranks 2..110 can change what is enacted with the argmax fixed. I ran that test: the first gate-passing candidate is M-bayesian-structure-learning with and without F_pi at every tau. BUT IT IS A VACUOUS TEST ON THIS FIELD -- 07-04 carries act-gate verdicts for 2 of 110 candidates and only one passes, so the first passer is invariant under ANY permutation, including one that reverses the field. Recorded as not-distinguishable, not as agreement. (3) MY OWN LIMIT, found while running (2): the persisted :rank does NOT equal ascending :G-total order on this trace (rank 1 is M-first-flights; min G-total is M-canon-fingerprint-store). July records carry :G-total but not :controller-score, which is what rank-actions actually sorted on. So both codex-22 measurement and my re-measurement order the field by a PROXY, not by the machine own ranking, and the 82-of-110 figure is a statement about that proxy. CORRECTED by claude-1, 2026-09-01, and the correction is sharper than what I wrote: the machine ORDERING is recorded -- :rank is the machine own rank, not a proxy -- and what is missing is the SCORE it was sorted on. rank-actions sorted on :controller-score, which July records do not carry; :G-total is a different quantity that happens to be present, agreeing with rank order at 2 of 110 positions. So the ordering is known and the effect of ADDING F_pi to it cannot be computed, because the base score F_pi would be added to is not in the record. Two consequences: H1b 50/50 is untouched, since it compares the recorded :rank and :decision against the realized policy and neither needs the missing score; and (b2) effect on selection is UNMEASURED on real data rather than measured as nil. This is precisely what I3 trace write exists to fix: a field recorded after it can answer the question at the machine own grain, and this one cannot."
   :slice-b2 "DONE and reviewed 2026-09-01. futon2 9b1c425 (codex-22). Flag :f-pi-policy-posterior?, default false; :f-pi-scaling :unscaled (B.9's form) or :by-tau. One seam only, softmax-weights, as its docstring requires. 34 tests / 134 assertions, clj-kondo clean, check-parens OK. Flag-off verified against policy.clj at 9b1c425^ loaded as a parallel namespace over five input classes: identical. THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: the ARGMAX DOES NOT CHANGE. On the 110-candidate field of 07-04 record 1 scored against record 2, at every tau I tried (0.1, 0.5, 1, 2, 5) and under both scalings, F_pi reshuffles between 20 and 106 of the 110 ranks -- and never changes which candidate is first. So F_pi is present, discriminating, and changes nothing the machine would do on the one field we can test. That is the J2 shape one level up: not computed-and-consumed-by-nothing, but consumed-with-no-effect-on-the-decision. It is a bound from ONE field, not a general claim; a second field could show otherwise, and until one does this term should not be described as changing selection. REVIEW FINDING: the delivered measurement reported unscaled and by-tau as separate results with identical numbers (82 moved, max 35). They were identical because it measured at the live tau, and the live tau is exactly 1.0 -- effective-temperature with the default selection gain 1.0 in :selection-gain-only mode -- where F/tau = F by construction. The comparison compared a thing with itself. Re-measured across tau: at 0.1 unscaled moves 20 ranks and by-tau 82; at 2.0 unscaled moves 106. The two are genuinely different objects. AND A PROPERTY WORTH THE ROW: under :by-tau with no habit prior the score is -(G + F_pi)/tau, a monotone transform of (G + F_pi), so the RANKING IS INVARIANT IN TAU -- 82 moved, max 35, at every tau tested. Under :unscaled tau trades G against F_pi and the ranking moves with it. So the scaling choice is not cosmetic: :by-tau makes F_pi influence exactly proportional to G and denies tau any power to balance them; :unscaled, which is what B.9 prints, does not. THE CHOICE IS CURRENTLY UNFALSIFIABLE ON LIVE DATA, which is the finding to carry: the live path holds tau at 1.0 because the selection gain never updates (C2, C454, fold-realized-outcome sim-only), and at tau = 1 the two scalings coincide exactly. Nothing on the live path can distinguish them until either the selection gain moves or J4 lands a gamma that does."
   :slice-b2-constraint "Before (b2) is dispatched: B.9 prints pi = sigma(ln E - F_pi - G) with NO temperature -- the paper has no tau. The WM's live posterior divides G by tau (policy.clj:244-246). Whether F_pi is divided by the same tau or enters unscaled is therefore a choice B.9 does not make for us, and it changes the size of the term (measured at about a twelfth of G unscaled). The row (b2) produces must STATE which, or its rank-change count is not attributable to anything. Raised by claude-1, 2026-09-01."
   :statement "Implement the per-policy free energy F_pi and include it in the policy posterior pi = sigma(ln E - F_pi - G) (parr2022 B.9), then retire the Laplace channel F at R8 once F_pi is consumed."
   :acceptance "efe.clj/policy.clj compute F_pi per candidate from the observations under that policy and Q(pi) includes it; a run record shows the term; the R8 scalar F is removed or its row re-derived from F_pi by a reviewed C row and D row; tests; clj-kondo clean; sha here."}
