   :review "Read 8668969. Logic is right and key-scoping is the correct choice (a file-scoped check would invalidate every signature on any unrelated edit and be learned as noise; key-scoping fails only when the signed content changed). Ran the negative control MYSELF: my first tamper did not land (sed anchor missed) and the check passed -- the ran-it-wrong case; verified the tamper by diff, re-ran: exit 1 naming C15, [:choices :gamma-fixed-point], d5e9c54, claude-1. Superseded-row skip requires the superseding row to exist -- right. Coverage count deliberately a superset (13 unchecked) -- right; an absence reported as success is the class this ledger catches. TWO FINDINGS FIXED BY THE REVIEWER (carve-out b): (1) paths were resolved against the caller cwd -- from the futon2 root the ../../../ slurp threw at line 66; from /tmp (how wm-edge-loop.sh calls it, absolute paths from an arbitrary cwd) git show ran outside the repo and the check reported the signed sha is not in this history -- a cwd problem wearing a history problem message, which would have stopped the loop. Now resolves the repo root from the script location (git rev-parse --show-toplevel :dir script-dir) and runs git show and the slurp against it; verified from wm-contract, futon2 root and /tmp; negative control re-run from /tmp fails correctly. (2) the default worklist path was cwd-relative; now the script directory. Retrofit note for C11, C12, C13: I authored them; claude-20 signed all three at d0ec018 -- that is the sha their :review-covers should carry, with :covers-key [:holes 0] (C11: the [:R6 :R16] entry is first), [:plumbing-notes :TRACE] (C12), [:choices :temperature-update :ruling] (C13)." :owner :claude-20
   :statement "claude-1 proposed turning the TN 9a rule -- a signature must not come to describe a later file state -- from something we each have to notice into something worklist_check.bb enforces. Implemented. A signed row names the entry it covers with :covers-key and the sha it read with :review-covers; the check reads the registry at that sha, compares that ONE key against the file today, and fails naming the row when they differ. Scoped by KEY rather than by file on purpose: a file-scoped check would invalidate every prior signature on the next unrelated registry edit, which would make the ledger unusable and teach everyone to ignore it. Superseded rows are skipped by name, since TN 9a says their signature is expected to be stale, and the superseding row must exist or the skip is just a word that switches the check off."
